          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Coherent states
            are members of special overcomplete families of elements (usually in the projectivization) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of states of certain systems in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hall+coherent+state"} Hall coherent state
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#of_the_harmonic_oscillator Of the harmonic oscillator
          %p
            The most classical coherent states of E. Schrödinger (1926,
            %em coherent
            refers to its meaning in optics) are the elements in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(\mathbb{R}^n)
            which are the eigenfunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |z\rangle = |z_1,\ldots,z_n\rangle
            of the annihilation operators
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1,\ldots,n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_i
            are complex numbers and all complex numbers appear in that way; more precisely the coherent states are parametrized by numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z=(z_1,\ldots,z_n)
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            providing the resolution of unity formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{\mathbb{C}^n} |z\rangle \langle z| d\mu = 1
          %p
            Coherent states however are not mutually orthogonal; they form an overcomplete family of states: not only that every vector in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
            can be expressed in terms of them but rather in more than one way and we can throw certain coherent states out and not to spoil the property (how many and which is a more subtle question). Thus expressing states in terms of coherent states is sort of Fourier analysis; in fact this approach is taken in a variant called wavelet analysis. Coherent states are minimizing the uncertainty relations, closest to classical states in their behaviour; their probability density is Gaussian. Vectors in the Hilbert space can be represented in the coherent state representation:
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi d
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |f\rangle = \int |z\rangle\langle z|f\rangle d\mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle z|f\rangle
            is a holomorphic function and this passage is called the Bargmann-Segal transform (referring to
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irving_Segal"} Irving Segal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bargmann-Fock+space"} Bargmann-Fock space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle z|f\rangle
            formally satisfies the classical Newton equations of motion.
          %p
            In general, projection operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |z\rangle \langle z|
            and the scalar measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
            do not separate, in fact instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |z\rangle \langle z| d\mu
            one considers more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+measure"} projection measure
            (descriptively a “measure” with values in self-adjoint projection operators on a separable Hilbert space) on a more general geometric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            replacing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            which is normalized to 1 on the whole space. Symbollically we can still pretend that the projective measure is of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) = \int_A |z\rangle \langle z| d\mu
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
            is an ordinary (scalar-valued) measure.
          %h3#over_a_
            Over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+manifold"} flag manifold
          %p
            The basic example of the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the homogeneous space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb {C}}/B = G/K
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a compact Lie group,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb{C}}
            its complexification,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\subset G
            maximal compact subgroup and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\subset G^{\mathbb{C}}
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+subgroup"} Borel subgroup
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+manifold"} flag manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/K
            is naturally a compact complex manifold, in fact Kähler. Fix a unitary character
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi:G\to S^1
            which uniquely extends to a character
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi:G^{\mathbb{C}}\to \mathbb{C}^*
            of the complexification; let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}_\chi
            be the corresponding 1-dimensional representation. Then let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi χ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi G
                        %mi ℂ
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\chi = G^{\mathbb{C}}\times_{G^{\mathbb{C}}} \mathbb{C}_\chi
            be the associated line bundle. It is equipped with a natural holomorphic structure and hermitean scalar product; the space of holomorphic (or antiholomorphic depending on conventions in the construction) sections of that bundle is finite-dimensional and irreducible by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel-Weil+theorem"} Borel-Weil theorem
            %strong Perelomov coherent states
            on that bundle are the elements of the orbit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of the heighest (equivalently lowest) weight vector (or equivalently of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb{C}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            in the space of a line bundle and then divide it by the value of the section on the projection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            to the homogeneous space. Up to a scalar, coherent states do not depend on the choice of the point on the line, hence this yields a holomorphic embedding of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\mathbb{C}}/B
            to the projectivization of the representation space, so called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong coherent states embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/K
